# Skill: performance

Use this skill when planning, writing, or reviewing performance / load tests with
**{{ performance | upper }}**.

## When to invoke

- The AC involves response time, throughput, or concurrent user targets
- Verifying that a new feature did not introduce a performance regression
- Running a load test before a major release or infrastructure change
- Investigating a timeout or latency complaint in production

## Types of performance tests

| Type | Purpose | When to use |
|---|---|---|
| **Smoke** | 1 VU, 1 iteration — confirms the script works | Before every load test run |
| **Load** | Expected normal traffic | Standard pre-release gate |
| **Stress** | Traffic beyond normal capacity | Find the breaking point |
| **Soak** | Normal traffic over a long period | Detect memory leaks, connection pool exhaustion |
| **Spike** | Sudden traffic surge | Flash sales, marketing campaigns |

Always run smoke → load → stress in that order. Never run stress as the first test.

{% if performance == 'k6' %}
## k6

### Minimal smoke test

```javascript
import http from "k6/http";
import { check, sleep } from "k6";

export const options = {
  vus: 1,
  iterations: 1,
};

export default function () {
  const res = http.get("https://your-app.example.com/api/health");
  check(res, { "status 200": (r) => r.status === 200 });
  sleep(1);
}
```

### Load test with thresholds

```javascript
export const options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: "1m", target: 50 },   // ramp up
    { duration: "3m", target: 50 },   // hold
    { duration: "1m", target: 0 },    // ramp down
  ],
  thresholds: {
    http_req_duration: ["p(95)<500"],  // 95% of requests under 500ms
    http_req_failed:   ["rate<0.01"],  // less than 1% errors
  },
};
```

### Run

```bash
k6 run tests/performance/smoke.js
k6 run tests/performance/load.js --out json=results/load.json
```

{% elif performance == 'locust' %}
## Locust

### Minimal smoke test

```python
from locust import HttpUser, task, between

class AppUser(HttpUser):
    wait_time = between(1, 3)

    @task
    def health_check(self):
        self.client.get("/api/health")
```

### Run

```bash
locust -f tests/performance/locustfile.py \
  --headless \
  --users 50 \
  --spawn-rate 5 \
  --run-time 5m \
  --host https://your-app.example.com
```

### Thresholds (fail CI on breach)

```bash
locust ... --exit-code-on-error 1 \
  --csv results/locust \
  # post-process results/locust_stats.csv to assert p95 < 500ms
```

{% elif performance == 'jmeter' %}
## JMeter

### Test plan structure

```
Thread Group
  ├── HTTP Request Sampler  (your endpoint)
  ├── Response Assertion    (status == 200)
  ├── Summary Report        (listeners)
  └── Aggregate Report
```

### Run headless

```bash
jmeter -n \
  -t tests/performance/load_test.jmx \
  -l results/results.jtl \
  -e -o results/html-report/
```

### Thresholds

Use the **Response Time Graph** listener or post-process `results.jtl` with:

```bash
# Assert p95 < 500ms using jtl-to-csv
python scripts/check_jmeter_thresholds.py results/results.jtl --p95 500
```

{% elif performance == 'gatling' %}
## Gatling

### Minimal simulation (Scala DSL)

```scala
class SmokeTest extends Simulation {
  val httpProtocol = http.baseUrl("https://your-app.example.com")

  val scenario = scenario("Smoke")
    .exec(http("health").get("/api/health").check(status.is(200)))

  setUp(scenario.inject(atOnceUsers(1))).protocols(httpProtocol)
    .assertions(global.responseTime.percentile(95).lt(500))
}
```

### Run

```bash
mvn gatling:test -Dgatling.simulationClass=SmokeTest
# or: gradle gatlingRun
```

{% elif performance == 'artillery' %}
## Artillery

### Minimal config (`load.yml`)

```yaml
config:
  target: "https://your-app.example.com"
  phases:
    - duration: 60
      arrivalRate: 10
  ensure:
    p95: 500
    maxErrorRate: 1

scenarios:
  - name: "Health check"
    flow:
      - get:
          url: "/api/health"
```

### Run

```bash
artillery run tests/performance/load.yml --output results/report.json
artillery report results/report.json
```

